CM_ SG_ 482 EPS_OK "Crosscheck 2026-08 vs qzwf/opendbc's independent RE of this same message (STEERING_MODULE_ADAS, bit 20): qzwf calls this bit STEER_REQ_ACTIVE_LOW (an inverse mirror of STEER_REQ at bit 21), not an EPS health flag. Every other signal in this message (STEER_ANGLE, COUNTER, CHECKSUM, STEER_REQ, and all SET_ME_* constants) matches qzwf's layout bit-for-bit. This bit feeds lkas_healthy in opendbc/car/byd/cam_lka/carstate.py - unresolved without a local capture, not changed here. See docs/BYD_ATTO3_COMMUNITY_LINEAGE.md.";
CM_ SG_ 814 ACCEL_CMD "CORRECTED 2026-09-21: was (1,-100) raw, with opendbc/car/byd/values.py applying ACCEL_MULT=26 on top, which put 1.30x the requested acceleration on the wire. Now carries the capture-derived scale directly, matching BYD_Atto3/DBC/byd_atto3.dbc's MPC_AccelerationCmd (0|8, x0.05, -5). Evidence: create_accel_command's +30 raw-count clip decodes to exactly +1.50 m/s2, the ceiling BYD_Atto3's COMMA-device captures recorded, and -80 to exactly -4.00; BYD_Atto3/DOC/jerk_brake_summary.md finds comfort/jerk values in 0.05 steps across every capture. qzwf/opendbc uses a third value (x16.67) and explicitly disclaims it as inferred, keeping ACC_CMD out of its TX allowlist. Raw TX envelope unchanged (raw 20..130); bench-validate before hardware use. See docs/BYD_ATTO3_QZWF_REFERENCE_PORT.md.";
CM_ BO_ 496 "DISPUTED 2026-09-21: this fork decodes WHEEL_SPEED as four 12-bit wheels at x0.1 plus fault bits 12-15. Two independent lineages decode the same frame as a SINGLE 16-bit speed at 0|16@1+ - BYD_Atto3/DBC/byd_atto3.dbc's ESP_VehicleSpeed (x1/14 km/h per count, from local CANape captures) and qzwf/opendbc's WHEELSPEED_CLEAN (x0.1, unused by their carstate). qzwf reads per-wheel speed from 0x122 instead and corrects x0.1 by 40/53 after measuring raw values ~32.5% high against the odometer, landing on 0.0755 - within 5.7% of byd_atto3.dbc's 1/14 = 0.0714, and both well below the x0.1 used here. carstate.parse_wheel_speeds, radar_interface and safety/modes/byd.h's UPDATE_VEHICLE_SPEED all read this frame, so vEgo and the speed the angle-rate lookup interpolates against are affected. Unresolved without a local GPS or odometer run - structure and scale both unchanged here pending one.";
CM_ SG_ 290 WHEELSPEED_BR "CROSSCHECK 2026-09-21: qzwf/opendbc declares this field identically (48|16@1+ x0.1) and then measured that it is wrong - 'byte 7 is a constant status byte (0x41), NOT the high byte of the wheel speed' - and derives rear-right from the other three wheels instead. Nothing in this fork reads WHEEL_SPEED_ALT today. Left as-is pending a local capture rather than guessing the real width. See docs/BYD_ATTO3_QZWF_REFERENCE_PORT.md.";
CM_ SG_ 790 SET_ME_XFF "DISPUTED 2026-09-21: bits 16-23 are not a constant. byd_han_dmev_2020.dbc (same address, ACC_MPC_STATE) and BYD_Atto3/DBC/byd_atto3.dbc both place a signed 11-bit LKAS_Output / MPC_LkasOutput at 16|11 here, with LKAS_ReqPrepare at 27 and LKAS_Active at 28 - and safety/modes/byd.h's mpc_lka path decodes exactly those bits as desired_torque. cam_lka/carstate.py reads this byte into lkas_hud_status_passthrough and create_lkas_hud writes it back out, so this fork is round-tripping the low byte of the camera's LKA torque output as filler. byd_atto3.dbc's CM_ SG_ 482 CONST_0xFF_B5 records qzwf/opendbc finding that treating a camera-derived field in the steering frame as don't-care silently killed their car's stock ADAS. qzwf's own 0x316 defines nothing in this region, so this is unresolved - not changed here.";
CM_ SG_ 482 UNKNOWN "CROSSCHECK 2026-09-21: this 14-bit field plus SET_ME_X01 and SET_ME_XE cover bits 0-19, which BYD_Atto3/DBC/byd_atto3.dbc decodes as two signed 10-bit steering angle-rate limits (MPC_SteerAngleRateUpper 0|10, MPC_SteerAngleRateLower 10|10). Decoding what create_can_steer_command actually transmits through that layout gives +299/-299 with SET_ME_XE=0xB and +299/-107 with 0xE, which also explains this file's own note that 0xB 'seem[s] to have the highest angle limit at high speed'. Independently, qzwf/opendbc measured the camera holding bytes 0-2 at 2b 55 eb - i.e. exactly the 0xB triple - for 1111 of 1121 STEER_REQ frames AT EVERY SPEED, and documents that varying these fields dropped the car's entire stock ADAS after tens of seconds. The 0xE-at-standstill variant in cam_lka/bydcan.py therefore puts a triple on the wire that no capture has seen; verify against a local capture before keeping it.";
